\lx malsan
\lsf
\ph
\ps
\pn
\ge
\de
\xv malsân 
\xe merchant
\so Rand 1888 p 168
\nt Two facts are  proved and illustrated by this word
Malsan.  1st. There were no men among the ancient 
Indians who lived by buying and selling.  This is proved
by their having adopted the  French word, for merchant,
(marchant),  which they invariably did when an object  was
introduced, unlike anything in use  among themselves.
2nd. It shows also shows what letters (rather sounds) they
used for substitutes for those lacking in their own
language. For r they used an l, and for ch (sh) they used
an s. Malsan for Marchand. And further it may be seen
how near two words may be etymologically, and at the
same time how far apart in appearance and pronunciation.
\dt 15/Apr/2024

\lx malsan
\lsf
