\nt There is no word in Micmac that means simply
father, or,  a father. When the word is used it is
joined with a possessive pronoun, thus: Nooch,
My  father; kooch, Thy father;  Ooochŭl, His father.
In the voc. case it is: Noo, My  father. To be a
father,  is, Wĕnjŭne, I have a child, and thus means
when used by a man, I am a father, and, when used
by a woman, I am a mother.
